From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Luke Craig <lacraig3@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 14:27:08 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] accel/tcg, target/arm: take SVC, UNDEF and aborts to EL1
 without the BQL

cpu_handle_exception() holds the BQL around every TCGCPUOps.do_interrupt.
On Arm that serializes every system call (SVC is delivered as EXCP_SWI),
and the exception return (ERET, and the AArch32 cpsr_write_eret) takes it
twice more for the EL change hooks.  An AArch64 guest's system-call rate
therefore does not grow with vCPUs under MTTCG: a getppid/close storm runs
at about 0.3 M calls/s at -smp 1, 2 and 4 alike, while x86, whose SYSCALL
is a helper that takes no lock, scales.

Add an optional TCGCPUOps.exception_bql_free(cpu): when it returns true
the cpu loop calls do_interrupt without the BQL.  Targets without it are
unchanged.

On Arm it is true for EXCP_SWI, EXCP_UDEF, EXCP_PREFETCH_ABORT and
EXCP_DATA_ABORT taken from EL0 or EL1 to EL1, when every registered EL
change hook says it would do nothing for that change.  Taking such an
exception writes only the vCPU's own state (ESR, FAR, ELR, SPSR, PSTATE,
SP, hflags; the AArch32 banked registers) and sets CPU_INTERRUPT_EXITTB,
which is atomic.  PSCI and semihosting calls (HVC, SMC, EXCP_SEMIHOST),
anything to EL2 or EL3, interrupts and debug exceptions keep the BQL, as
does every other caller of arm_cpu_do_interrupt (they hold it already).

EL change hooks get an optional idle predicate, called on the vCPU's
thread without the BQL:
- PMU: idle while PMCNTENSET is zero.  pmu_op_start() then only records
  each counter's underlying count, pmu_op_finish() does nothing, and
  every reader of a counter or its delta calls pmu_op_start() first.
- GICv3 CPU interface: idle for changes between EL0 and EL1; what it
  computes depends on the EL only through EL2, EL3 and the security
  state, which EL0 and EL1 share.
- A hook without a predicate (the RME generic-timer hook) is never idle.

The exception returns use the same test and skip the BQL and the hooks
when all are idle.  arm_cpu_do_interrupt asks again after the consumers
of the engine's system-call entry (A32 SVC) and of the syscall trap have
run, and takes the BQL if the answer changed.

